            <div xml:id="d1520">

                <pb facs="1142" n="1026" sameAs="frus:frus1915/pg1026"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 812.404/62.</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to
                        Consul Schmutz</hi>.</head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 90.]</seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Department of State</hi>, <lb/>
                            <hi rendition="#italic">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1915-04-01">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">April 1, 1915</hi></date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: The Department has received your despatch
                    No. 258 of February 12, 1915, reporting that three Spanish priests [etc.]</p>
                <p>The Department approves of your action in sending the telegram to Mr. George C.
                    Carothers asking him to see General Villa and try to have the order
                    rescinded.</p>
                <p>It also approves of your action in giving certain letters to the Spanish Vice
                    Consul for presentation to the three consular officers between Aguascalientes
                    and El Paso, should it be necessary to request any aid of them.</p>
                <p>I am [etc.]</p>
                <closer>For the Secretary of State: <lb/>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Wilbur J. Carr.</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
